@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Accordion's Overview, written by hand after the selection and feedback Overviews: variants, one
   open at a time, rich headers and content, sizes, forms in sections (previewed in each mode) and
   following an item from C#. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>An accordion is a stack of sections, each with a title the user clicks to open or close it. It suits sections the user opens one by one, such as FAQs or settings. Each item is a native <Code>details</Code> element, so the browser opens and closes it, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For a single "show more", see <Anchor Href="docs/components/collapsible">Collapsible</Anchor>. For views the user switches between, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/tabs">Tabs</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>A variant sets how the items are framed. <Code>Separated</Code> (the default) shows each item as its own card, <Code>Bordered</Code> puts them in one bordered box, and <Code>Flush</Code> sets them flush with the page. Set <Code>Variant</Code> to choose one.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">One open at a time</Heading>
<TextBlock>An exclusive accordion keeps one item open: opening another item closes the one that was open. It suits steps or answers the user reads one at a time. Set <Code>Exclusive</Code> to turn it on. The browser does the closing, so it works with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-exclusive" />
<TextBlock>To stop an item from opening, set <Code>Disabled</Code> on it. Exclusive items share a <Code>name</Code>. To group several accordions so that one item is open across all of them, set the same <Code>Name</Code> on each.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Rich headers and content</Heading>
<TextBlock>An item's title can hold more than text, such as a badge, and its content can hold text, buttons and lists. For a title with more than text, use an <Code>AccordionHeader</Code>. To show an icon before the title, set <Code>Icon</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-content" />
<TextBlock>The content is a column with a gap, so text, buttons and lists need no wrapper. To change the content's spacing, set <Code>ContentPadding</Code> and <Code>ContentGap</Code> on the accordion for every item, or on one item. To run a table or image edge to edge, set <Code>ContentPadding="0"</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes</Heading>
<TextBlock>The size scales the titles and spacing together. <Code>Sm</Code> suits sidebars and dense pages, and <Code>Lg</Code> suits FAQ pages. Set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-sizes" />

<Heading Level="2">Forms in sections</Heading>
<TextBlock>An accordion can hold a form in each section, as a settings page does. After a form post, the section whose form posted should be open. Set <Code>Open</Code> on that item to open it from the server.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-settings" />
<TextBlock>The browser keeps track of which items are open, not the server. On static pages, after every form post or reload, the items set to <Code>Open</Code> are open and the rest are closed. Open the section that posted whether it saved or not (in <Code>OnInvalidSubmit</Code> too), as this example does, so its message or errors are in view.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Binding the open state</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, you can read and set whether an item is open from C#. Set <Code>@@bind-Open</Code> on an <Code>AccordionItem</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="accordion-bound" />

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Accordion>
            <AccordionItem Title="How do refunds work?" Open>
                Refunds go back to the original card within 5 business days.
            </AccordionItem>
            <AccordionItem Title="Can I change my plan later?">
                Yes. Changes apply from your next billing date.
            </AccordionItem>
        </Accordion>
        """;
}
